   "text": "Let $A = \\begin{bmatrix} B & 0 \\\\ 0 & C \\end{bmatrix}$, where $B$ and $C$ are square. Show that $A$ is invertible if and only if both $B$ and $C$ are invertible.",
   "midtermProof": [],
   "answer": "",
   "solution": "Let $B$ be $k\\times k$ and $C$ be $m\\times m$, so $A$ is $(k+m)\\times(k+m)$.\n\n($\\Leftarrow$) If $B$ and $C$ are invertible, then\n$$\\begin{bmatrix} B & 0 \\\\ 0 & C \\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix} B^{-1} & 0 \\\\ 0 & C^{-1} \\end{bmatrix} = \\begin{bmatrix} I_k & 0 \\\\ 0 & I_m \\end{bmatrix} = \\begin{bmatrix} B^{-1} & 0 \\\\ 0 & C^{-1} \\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix} B & 0 \\\\ 0 & C \\end{bmatrix},$$\nso $A$ is invertible with $A^{-1} = \\begin{bmatrix} B^{-1} & 0 \\\\ 0 & C^{-1} \\end{bmatrix}$.\n\n($\\Rightarrow$) Suppose $A$ is invertible, and partition $A^{-1} = \\begin{bmatrix} P & Q \\\\ R & T \\end{bmatrix}$ conformally, with $P$ of size $k\\times k$ and $T$ of size $m\\times m$. Then\n$$AA^{-1} = \\begin{bmatrix} BP & BQ \\\\ CR & CT \\end{bmatrix} = I \\quad\\text{and}\\quad A^{-1}A = \\begin{bmatrix} PB & QC \\\\ RB & TC \\end{bmatrix} = I.$$\nComparing diagonal blocks gives $BP = I_k = PB$ and $CT = I_m = TC$. So $B$ is invertible with inverse $P$, and $C$ is invertible with inverse $T$. $\\blacksquare$"
