   "setup": "Suppose $M = \\begin{bmatrix} A & B \\\\ C & D \\end{bmatrix}$, with $A = \\begin{bmatrix} 1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\end{bmatrix}$, $C = \\begin{bmatrix} 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 \\end{bmatrix}$, $B = \\begin{bmatrix} -3 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 \\end{bmatrix}$, $D = \\begin{bmatrix} 1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 4 \\end{bmatrix}$. Let $R_1$ be the row-block $[A \\; B]$ and $R_2$ the row-block $[C \\; D]$.\n\n(a) Perform the row-block operation $M \\xrightarrow{(-CA^{-1})R_1 + R_2} N$.\n(b) Perform the row-block operation $M \\xrightarrow{(-BD^{-1})R_2 + R_1} S$.",
   "context": "",
   "contextLabel": "",
   "text": "Find a matrix $E$ such that $EM = S$.",
   "midtermProof": [],
   "answer": "$$E = \\begin{bmatrix} I & -BD^{-1} \\\\ 0 & I \\end{bmatrix} = \\begin{bmatrix} 1 & 0 & -6 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & \\tfrac12 & -\\tfrac12 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 1 \\end{bmatrix}$$",
