   "setup": "Let $M = \\begin{bmatrix} A & B \\\\ C & D \\end{bmatrix} \\in M_n$, where $A \\in M_p$ and $D \\in M_q$. In class we proved that if $A$ is invertible, then $\\det M = (\\det A)\\det(D - CA^{-1}B)$.",
   "context": "",
   "contextLabel": "",
   "text": "Use this formula to compute $\\det M$, where $A = \\begin{bmatrix} 0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\end{bmatrix}$, $B = \\begin{bmatrix} -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 \\end{bmatrix}$, $C = \\begin{bmatrix} 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1 \\end{bmatrix}$, $D = \\begin{bmatrix} 1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1 \\end{bmatrix}$.",
   "midtermProof": [],
   "answer": "$\\det M = 0$.",
   "solution": "$\\det M=(\\det A)\\det(M/A)$, where $M/A=D-CA^{-1}B$. $\\det A = 1$ and $A^{-1}=\\begin{bmatrix} 0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0 \\end{bmatrix}$. Then\n$$CA^{-1}=\\begin{bmatrix} -1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\end{bmatrix},\\qquad CA^{-1}B=\\begin{bmatrix} 1 & 8 \\\\ -1 & 0 \\end{bmatrix},\\qquad M/A = D-CA^{-1}B=\\begin{bmatrix} 0 & -7 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\end{bmatrix}.$$\n$\\det(M/A)=0\\cdot 1-(-7)\\cdot 0=0$, so $\\det M=(\\det A)\\det(M/A)=1\\cdot 0=0$."
