   "answer": "$XY=\\begin{bmatrix} 1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 4 & 5 & 6 \\\\ 7 & 8 & 9 \\end{bmatrix}$, which has rank $2$; $X$ ($3\\times 2$) has rank $2$ and $Y$ ($2\\times 3$) has rank $2$, so this is a full-rank factorization.",
   "solution": "$$\\begin{bmatrix} 2 & 3 \\\\ 5 & 6 \\\\ 8 & 9 \\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix} 2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 1 \\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix} 1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 4 & 5 & 6 \\\\ 7 & 8 & 9 \\end{bmatrix}=:A.$$\nThe rows of $A$ satisfy row$_3=2\\,$row$_2-$row$_1$, and rows 1 and 2 are independent, so $\\operatorname{rank}A=2$.\n- $X$ has full column rank: its top $2\\times 2$ minor is $\\det\\begin{bmatrix} 2 & 3 \\\\ 5 & 6 \\end{bmatrix}=-3\\neq 0$, so its 2 columns are independent.\n- $Y$ has full row rank: $\\det\\begin{bmatrix} 2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0 \\end{bmatrix}=1\\neq 0$ (columns 1–2), so its 2 rows are independent.\n\nSince $r=\\operatorname{rank}A=2$ matches the inner dimension, $A=XY$ is a full-rank factorization."
