   "solution": "Sizes: $A,B\\in M_{m\\times k}$. Let $r=\\dim\\operatorname{col}A=\\dim\\operatorname{col}B$ (equal because the spaces are equal), so $\\operatorname{rank}A=\\operatorname{rank}B=r$. Assume $r\\ge 1$.\n\nPick a basis $x_1,\\dots,x_r$ of $\\operatorname{col}A=\\operatorname{col}B$; it is the same subspace, so one basis serves both. Put $X=[x_1\\,\\cdots\\,x_r]\\in M_{m\\times r}$; its columns are independent, so $X$ has full column rank. Each column of $A$ and of $B$ lies in $\\operatorname{col}X$, so it equals $X$ times its coefficient vector in $\\mathbb{F}^r$; placing these coefficient vectors side by side gives $A=XY_1$ and $B=XY_2$ with $Y_1,Y_2\\in M_{r\\times k}$. Moreover $r=\\operatorname{rank}(XY_1)\\le\\operatorname{rank}Y_1\\le r$, so $Y_1$ (and likewise $Y_2$) has full row rank $r$."
