   "text": "Let $v_1 = \\begin{bmatrix} 1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1 \\end{bmatrix}$, $v_2 = \\begin{bmatrix} 0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1 \\end{bmatrix}$. Extend $v_1, v_2$ into a basis for $\\mathbb{R}^4$.",
   "midtermProof": [],
   "answer": "$\\{v_1,v_2,e_3,e_4\\}$ is a basis of $\\mathbb{R}^4$, where $e_3=(0,0,1,0)^T$ and $e_4=(0,0,0,1)^T$ (not unique).",
   "solution": "$[v_1\\ v_2\\ e_3\\ e_4]$ is lower triangular with diagonal entries $1$, so\n$$\\det[v_1\\ v_2\\ e_3\\ e_4]=\\det\\begin{bmatrix} 1 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0 & 1 \\end{bmatrix}=1\\neq 0.$$\nSo the four vectors are linearly independent, and 4 independent vectors in $\\mathbb{R}^4$ form a basis."
